4.82.m:
In your tenth past birth, desiring to worship Acyuta,
you fasted on the auspicious dvādaśī that brings victory to good deeds, O fortunate one;
and by its grace, the sin that is exceedingly hard to conquer

One should avoid lust, anger, greed, arrogance, and delusion;
one should avoid treachery and all other faults born of the pride of wealth. / A devotee of Viṣṇu should also contemplate the worthlessness of worldly existence.
With a mind cultivated in this manner, desiring the welfare of living beings,

4.83.m:
By fasting observances, the goddess, and by various kinds of restrictions [worshipped him];
after a long time, the Garuḍa-bannered lord became pleased with her;
and Acyuta lifted her up and established her in her proper place.

'The sacred waters within the cosmic egg are touched by your rays, O Sun;
they become pure for the virtuous.' He should then touch the clay again.
— Thus the mantra for looking at the Sun.

In their midst he should set up a pedestal draped with cloth;
upon it he should place a vessel made of gold, silver, copper, or wood,
filled with water, and then place [an image] in its center.

4.86.m:
Otherwise, miscarriage or obstruction takes place;
therefore, you should conduct yourself in this manner during this pregnancy.
A handsome son, beautiful in all his limbs, will be born.
